":[41,42],"#Application of Artificial Intelligence to Lipid Nutrition: A Narrative Review#🟠 Limitasyonlar (Turuncu)#{1}":[42,42]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Görüntüle","target":"zotero://select/library/items/8P2H5XKH","line":22},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=1&annotation=PJ2EUWDM","line":33},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=1&annotation=XMBLJDLX","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 10","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=10&annotation=Y9FPKR6Z","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 3","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=3&annotation=53ZCSSRV","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 7","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=7&annotation=JRTWQV59","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 7","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=7&annotation=TGHCP67K","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=1&annotation=FFHQCP2M","line":39},{"title":"Sayfa 11","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=11&annotation=7CDMG8RT","line":39},{"title":"Sayfa 11","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=11&annotation=XULGG6WH","line":39},{"title":"Sayfa 10","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/ZLS2ZHBU?page=10&annotation=R8TBN2MJ","line":42}],"metadata":{"Title":"Application of Artificial Intelligence to Lipid Nutrition: A Narrative Review","Authors":"Elena N. Naumova, Andrea Hsieh, Rinat Rivka Ran‐Ressler, Chang Woon Jang, Samantha L. Huey, Fabiola Dionisi","Year":2025,"DOI":"10.1002/lipd.70000","Nitelik":null,"Alan":null,"Konu":null,"AI_Methodology":null,"Layer_Focus":null,"Model_Asamasi":null,"Proposed_Tool":null,"Methods":"his narrative review highlights AI applications in medicine and pharmaceuticals through a nutrition lens and examines emerging opportunities in lipid nutrition. Beginning at the molecular level with nutrient interactions, lipidomics, and modes and mechanisms of action (MoA), we describe potential applications of AI tools at the community level and global level for personalized nutrition, health claims, product development, and ultimately predictions for public health.","Findings":"databases. AI methodologies are well-positioned to transform nutrition research and practice by assisting in data collection, processing, and analysis; monitoring nutritional status of populations; elucidating MoA; predicting bioactive compounds, nutrients, and the health effects of diets; and facilitating nutrition recommendations tailored to individualsions in medicine and drug discovery that can inspire the use of AI in lipid nutrition are concentrated in four fields: • Screening of large chemical (drugs) libraries, identifying disease-associated targets, and analyzing preclinical and clinical data (Turabi et al. 2022; Gao et al. 2021) • Design and optimization of preclinical and clinical trials, predicting potential drug targets, binding affinities of drug candidates, off-target effects, toxicity, patient responses, adverse events, modeling pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics to predict clinical outcomes (Wang, Qiu, et al. 2022; Wang, Bouzembrak, et al. 2022) • Understanding the MoA, a key step to drug discovery and repurposing and the key challenge related to using the right data in the right format and for the intended purpose (Palermo  2023; Bender and Cortés-Ciriano  2021a, 2021b, Part 1 and 2) • Understanding animal and human metabolism, from genes to metabolites, expressed phenotypes, therapeutic impact, toxicology, and safetyAI and ML techniques can identify clinically relevant biomarkers from lipidomics data, enabling the early diagnosis of various diseases. Table S2 summarizes recent studies published from the past 4 years that utilize popular ML techniques in lipidomics investigationUsing ML to identify clinical lipids offers several advantages. Automated ML models can process vast amounts of data quickly, providing timely predictions that can aid in early diagnosis and intervention.  The integration of ML in predicting clinical lipids demonstrates significant potential in enhancing early diagnosis and intervention for lipid-related disorders.Both successful and unsuccessful attempts to apply AI tools to the growing field of lipid research and product development reveal the critical needs for (a) terminology, concepts, and data standardization, documentation, and harmonization; (b) data availability, accessibility, and credibility; (c) metrics for models' performance, scalability, interoperability, and interpretability; (d) better understanding the sources of algorithmic biases and implications of data bias for model training; and (e) addressing data privacy concerns and regulatory compliance.","Conclusions":"With this review, we encourage further research to advance innovation through the integration of AI and lipid nutrition for the benefit of public health.AI technologies enable a comprehensive characterization and understanding of functional ingredients and molecules. The use of AI can expand the portfolio of bioactives available to the food industry, addressing unmet consumer needs and placing them at the center of innovatiAI-powered tools have the potential to transform nutrition science and health outcomes, but should be carefully considered in the context of benefits and ongoing and emerging challenges. To address these challenges, it is essential to develop, compile, and verify relevant, curated, and high-quality data tailored to the specific application and ensuring the interconnectivity and compatibility of different dataset","Limitations":"Several challenges and limitations in applying ML to lipidomics were noted. ML models require large and diverse datasets for training and validation. The analysis of lipids can be constrained by sample size and variability, particularly in specific disease contexts or rare lipid species. This can impact the performance and generalizability of ML models. ML models, particularly deep learning models, are often considered as black boxes, making it challenging to interpret the underlying biological mechanisms and lipidomics patterns identified by the models.","User_Notes":""},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[],"last_embed":{"hash":"vkcxke","at":1774939666413}},"smart_blocks:naumovaApplicationArtificialIntelligence2025a.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.07180157,0.03427281,0.01382906,0.00515346,-0.01167934,-0.00506799,0.03091416,0.05779932,-0.00968293,-0.02696056,-0.02074642,-0.07547078,0.01054196,0.02505025,0.06354711,-0.03741029,-0.01542921,0.01222815,-0.03853344,-0.01691559,0.07910629,-0.02369251,0.02209842,-0.00306572,0.01299029,0.04438043,-0.03440583,-0.040761,-0.07969368,-0.24018367,0.00232983,0.00261513,0.07896214,-0.02069616,-0.04510746,0.03940555,0.00423198,0.08011914,-0.01208512,0.03887255,0.06930467,0.0096795,0.0148562,0.01345524,0.0291306,-0.05065313,-0.03683753,0.02186059,-0.06053679,-0.02607192,-0.0079959,-0.04471658,0.01656061,0.07055714,0.05318801,0.01809035,0.01543418,0.0499649,0.04432692,0.09466683,0.0211522,0.05989297,-0.20463797,0.11840983,0.06146745,0.01278659,-0.04774671,-0.00825014,0.03660715,0.00149385,-0.03132555,0.01792162,0.04861925,-0.00679381,0.02171274,0.04657514,0.05282858,-0.05467677,0.01354561,-0.04510838,-0.00091423,0.01235962,-0.03238457,-0.00158191,-0.07683987,-0.00310129,-0.02643419,-0.06784792,0.06981111,-0.03007398,-0.0050553,-0.00899028,-0.03070646,0.03414601,-0.0483999,0.00572491,-0.01573483,-0.02890279,-0.03591876,0.09438182,-0.00149944,-0.02059713,-0.0333317,-0.02433019,0.00755335,-0.03261915,-0.05213093,-0.09789668,0.01119888,0.05862633,0.01543219,0.02154777,-0.01800703,-0.01569912,-0.03517041,0.02121304,0.10211142,0.03062479,0.01332746,-0.04730257,-0.03669082,0.00944632,0.02356718,0.0122415,-0.00389304,0.01456164,0.00273942,0.07170284,-0.04923193,-0.02691419,0.07008316,0.03196432,-0.0822471,0.00021976,0.00233541,-0.00673353,